ause  white has some really good options one  they could just push forward with  d5 and once you have played this  move d6 now all of a sudden you can  bring your bishop to  b5 for whites pinning down the knight to  the king  yes the bishop here on c8 is now  attacking  this f5 square but i feel like black can  get into  some pretty tricky situations even if  white takes here on  f5 maybe black recaptures here but then  bishop to  b5 pinning down the knights maybe play  a6 to attack it but then after the  exchange right here  the pawn structure is all beat up pawn  takes here on e5  black recaptures you can see the queens  come off the board you can see double  pawns here  on the c file for black so definitely  not exactly the position  i would like uh and so for that reason  i do think it's easier just to go ahead  and take instead of playing d6 go ahead  and take with your pawn here on e4  they're probably gonna take with their  knight don't see another option that  they really have  and then to play queen to f6  white has a few options from here  probably the best move just go and take  the knights here on  c6 black can take with their pawn this  opens up for  the dark square bishop it also allows  black to play  d5 and have a pretty good pawn structure  in the center so that's why you would be  taking with your b-pawn here  to c6 and then you know if they ever  tried something queen two  h5 check i can't really do too much  without the knight supporting that  because you could just play g6 and  that's going to stop the  attack now if they decide okay they  don't want to take  with their knight here on uh c6  maybe they play queen to c2  attacking the pawn right here let me  just go ahead and play  a d5 up protecting this we have two  central pawns uh they don't so we're in  a pretty good position  right here the other option might be  bishop to b5  but this is going to be a mistake  because with our pawn is here on d7  then bringing the bishop out and you're  not really pinning down the knight to  anything  we just go ahead and take with our  knight here on e5 after they take with  our pawn  we can take with our queen and they're  going to be down  in material so that's what's going to  happen uh if they play the most natural  move  i'm just taking here knight to e5 we're  just going to bring our queen here  to f6  now that was if they play d4 against the  counter gambit but they could play  something else you know bishop to  a b5 maybe they could try but just as we  looked at in the previous example  if they bring their bishop out and it's  not pinning down the knight  this isn't going to do too much for them  we could take with our pawn right here  let's say they take with their bishop  here  well we could just take their bishop  they could play  knight 2 e5 we could play queen 2 g5  this is attacking both the knight  and this pawn here on a g2 there's no  way for them to really  defend this and this pawn here on e4 is  also a nuisance then we can play knight  to f6 at some point  uh we can eventually get our light  square bishop involvement into the game  castle on the queen side so  just know they don't have a lot of good  options bringing the bishop out here  is not going to do anything so what  other options do they have  in their other video we've already  looked at if they take here we've  already looked at if they play  pawn to d4 then they could play somewhat  defensive  they may have come out the gates  thinking that they're going to push  forward and then when we attack the pawn  here on e4  they get a little timid and play d3  supporting this pawn on e4 because they  really don't want you to take  and attack the knight here on f3  so that is absolutely an option from  here because they're playing timid i  like to be extremely aggressive  so i'm going to be looking to push here  on d5  really looking to control the center of  the board  so let's look at all the options that  they may have from here they could take  our  f pawn from here we're just going to  take back with  our bishop right here and we have lots  of different options depending on where  they go we could play knight to  f6 we could get our dark square bishop  involved into the game get our queen  involved into the game to a lot of  different squares castle on  the queen side but you can see here we  have our two central pawns  they have one central pawn we're not  down upon usually when you play a gambit  you're down  in material here your knight on c6 is  protecting your e5 pawn which is being  attacked right now  so you have a very strong setup and you  are controlling the center of the board  definitely a position i like for black  they could also take our other pawn  so take him right here and attacking the  knight here on  c6 we're gonna have to be doing  something but we can just take it with  our queen that's fine  and if they push forward because how do  they really attack right here black is  controlling the center of the board  if they play c4 then i like just  bringing the queen back here to  f7 you have different options you could  play queen to  d6 the main reason that i like queen to  f7  is because this allows me to get my  light square bishop involved into the  game  castle on the queen side and it frees up  this semi-open d  file so that eventually when your rook  comes here to d8  it can attack this entire foul right  here you can see this pawn is going to  be under attack  for black for a while here so it just  gives you a lot of attacking  lines and so that's why queen to f7 well  it may look like a weird square that you  don't bring your queen to that often  in this opening it does do extremely  well if they don't take one of your  pawns  uh tough to find another move that i  think they would play but maybe queen  two a4  uh once you play that d-pawn as we said  that's going to allow them to pin  down your knights they can't bring their  light scored bishop because they are  blocking it by d3 so d3 definitely not  the best move for them  but queen to a4 this is threatening  knight taking here on e5  so that does definitely pose a threat we  have to deal with but then just queen to  d7  really takes everything out of their  attack  hopefully everyone enjoyed this video on  the decline lines  just don't see too many options these  are the the ones i think you may see the  most here  but definitely wanted to make this video  as i did get requests on the last video  always look at the comments to see what  types of videos you want to see  and there's definitely a lot of people  saying hey love the counter gambit but  do want to see the decline line so thank  you guys so much for watching and  supporting the channel  and i will see you in the next video

